1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
Section 4

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:02,362
Hi.

3
00:00:02,362 --> 00:00:07,179
Today we're going to be looking at animals in urban environments

4
00:00:07,179 --> 00:00:13,455
and I'm going to be telling you about some research on how they're affected

5
00:00:13,455 --> 00:00:14,800
by these environments.

6
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:22,733
Now, in evolutionary terms, urban environments represent huge upheavals, the sorts of massive changes

7
00:00:22,733 --> 00:00:26,700
that usually happen over millions of years.

8
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:32,037
And we used to think that only a few species could adapt to this

9
00:00:32,037 --> 00:00:32,800
new environment.

10
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:38,650
One species which is well known as being highly adaptable is the crow,

11
00:00:38,650 --> 00:00:44,500
and there've been various studies about how they manage to learn new skills.

12
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:46,941
Another successful species is the pigeon,

13
00:00:46,941 --> 00:00:52,638
because they're able to perch on ledges on the walls of city buildings, just

14
00:00:52,638 --> 00:00:56,300
like they once perched on cliffs by the sea.

15
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:02,119
But in fact, we're now finding that these early immigrants were just the start

16
00:01:02,119 --> 00:01:05,859
of a more general movement of animals into cities,

17
00:01:05,859 --> 00:01:09,600
and of adaptation by these animals to city life.

18
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:16,552
And one thing that researchers are finding especially interesting is the speed with which

19
00:01:16,552 --> 00:01:22,510
they're doing this - we're not talking about gradual evolution here - these animals

20
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:24,000
are changing fast.

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,282
Let me tell you about some of the studies that have been carried out

22
00:01:28,282 --> 00:01:29,200
in this area.

23
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:34,535
So, in the University of Minnesota, a biologist called Emilie Snell-Rood

24
00:01:34,535 --> 00:01:40,840
and her colleagues looked at specimens of urbanised small mammals such as mice

25
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:44,720
and gophers that had been collected in Minnesota,

26
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:48,600
and that are now kept in museums there.

27
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:54,078
And she looked at specimens that had been collected over the last hundred years,

28
00:01:54,078 --> 00:01:57,600
which is a very short time in evolutionary terms.

29
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:03,518
And she found that during that time, these small mammals had experienced a jump

30
00:02:03,518 --> 00:02:06,900
in brain size when compared to rural mammals.

31
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:11,061
Now, we can't be sure this means they're more intelligent,

32
00:02:11,061 --> 00:02:16,887
but since the sizes of other parts of the body didn't change, it does

33
00:02:16,887 --> 00:02:19,800
suggest that something cognitive was going on.

34
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:25,556
And Snell-Rood thinks that this change might reflect the cognitive demands of adjusting to

35
00:02:25,556 --> 00:02:30,900
city life - having to look in different places to find food, for example,

36
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:34,600
and coping with a whole new set of dangers.

37
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:41,652
Then over in Germany at the Max Planck Institute, there's another biologist called Catarina

38
00:02:41,652 --> 00:02:48,200
Miranda who's done some experiments with blackbirds living in urban and rural areas.

39
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:53,900
And she's been looking not at their anatomy but at their behaviour.

40
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:59,118
So as you might expect, she's found that the urban blackbirds tend to be

41
00:02:59,118 --> 00:03:03,964
quite bold - they're prepared to face up to a lot of threats that

42
00:03:03,964 --> 00:03:06,200
would frighten away their country counterparts.

43
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:12,957
But there's one type of situation that does seem to frighten the urban blackbirds,

44
00:03:12,957 --> 00:03:17,300
and that's anything new - anything they haven't experienced before.

45
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:19,250
And if you think about it,

46
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:22,500
that's quite sensible for a bird living in the city.

47
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:29,335
Jonathan Atwell, in Indiana University, is looking at how a range of animals respond

48
00:03:29,335 --> 00:03:30,800
to urban environments.

49
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:37,281
He's found that when they're under stress, their endocrine systems react by reducing the

50
00:03:37,281 --> 00:03:41,448
amount of hormones such as corticosterone into their blood.

51
00:03:41,448 --> 00:03:43,300
It's a sensible-seeming adaptation.

52
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:49,425
A rat that gets scared every time a subway train rolls past won't be

53
00:03:49,425 --> 00:03:50,300
very successful.

54
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:56,022
There's just one more study I'd like to mention which is by Sarah Partan

55
00:03:56,022 --> 00:03:57,248
and her team,

56
00:03:57,248 --> 00:04:02,152
and they've been looking at how squirrels communicate in an urban environment,

57
00:04:02,152 --> 00:04:07,874
and they've found that a routine part of their communication is carried out by

58
00:04:07,874 --> 00:04:09,100
waving their tails.

59
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:12,474
You do also see this in the country,

60
00:04:12,474 --> 00:04:18,378
but it's much more prevalent in cities, possibly because it's effective in a noisy

61
00:04:18,378 --> 00:04:18,800
environment.

62
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,067
So what are the long-term implications of this?

63
00:04:23,067 --> 00:04:30,000
One possibility is that we may see completely new species developing in cities.

64
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,950
But on the other hand, it's possible that not all of these adaptations will

65
00:04:35,950 --> 00:04:36,800
be permanent.

66
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:42,633
Once the animal's got accustomed to its new environment, it may no longer need

67
00:04:42,633 --> 00:04:44,300
the features it's developed.

68
00:04:44,300 --> 00:04:49,000
So, now we've had a look ...
